149
00:13:46,620 --> 00:13:51,940
Industrialization came to the United States after 1790, as North American

150
00:13:51,940 --> 00:13:57,800
entrepreneurs increased productivity by reorganizing work and building factories.

151
00:13:58,280 --> 00:14:03,900
These innovations in manufacturing boosted output and living standards to an

152
00:14:03,900 --> 00:14:10,440
unprecedented extent. The average per capita wealth increased by nearly 1 per year,

153
00:14:10,980 --> 00:14:13,560
30 over the course of a generation.

154
00:14:14,440 --> 00:14:18,740
Goods had once been luxury items became part of everyday life.

155
00:14:19,700 --> 00:14:24,780
The impressive gain in output stemmed primarily from the change of the way in which

156
00:14:24,780 --> 00:14:26,260
workers made goods.

157
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:32,420
Since the 1790s, North American entrepreneurs, even without technological

158
00:14:32,420 --> 00:14:37,060
improvements, had broadened the scope of the work system that made manufacturing

159
00:14:37,060 --> 00:14:42,300
more efficient by distributing materials to a succession of workers who each

160
00:14:42,300 --> 00:14:45,660
performed a single step of the production process.

161
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:53,160
For example, during the 1820s and 1830s, the shoe industry greatly expanded the

162
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:55,480
scale and extent of the work system.

163
00:14:55,480 --> 00:15:00,780
Tens of thousands of rural women, paid according to the amount they produced,

164
00:15:01,400 --> 00:15:06,860
fabricated the uppers of shoes, which were bound to the soles by skilled shoemakers

165
00:15:06,860 --> 00:15:13,420
in dozens of Massachusetts towns, whereas previously skilled shoemakers would have

166
00:15:13,420 --> 00:15:14,800
made the entire shoe.

167
00:15:15,380 --> 00:15:20,960
This system of production made the employer a powerful shoe boss, and eroded

168
00:15:20,960 --> 00:15:24,440
workers' control over the pace and conditions of labor.

169
00:15:24,440 --> 00:15:30,620
However, it also dramatically increased the output of shoes while cutting the price.

170
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:37,780
Entrepreneurs created an even more important new organization, the Modern Factory,

171
00:15:38,200 --> 00:15:43,560
which used power -driven machines and assembly line techniques to turn out large

172
00:15:43,560 --> 00:15:45,460
quantities of well -made goods.

173
00:15:45,840 --> 00:15:52,580
As early as 1782, the creative inventor Oliver Evans had built a highly automated

174
00:15:52,580 --> 00:15:56,260
labor -saving flour mill driven by water power.

175
00:15:56,780 --> 00:16:01,660
His machinery lifted the grain to the top of the cleaned it as it fell into

176
00:16:01,660 --> 00:16:03,500
containers known as hoppers,

177
00:16:04,180 --> 00:16:09,360
ground the grain into flour, and then conveyed the flour back to the top of the mill

178
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:13,120
to allow it to cool as it descended into barrels.

179
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:19,580
Subsequently, manufacturers made use of new improved stationary steam engines to

180
00:16:19,580 --> 00:16:20,960
power their mills.

181
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:27,180
This new technology enabled them to build factories in the nation's largest cities,

182
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:33,320
taking advantage of urban inexpensive labor, good transportation networks, and eager

