Question 19. Recording 2 The economic depression in the late 19th century United

190
00:18:00,980 --> 00:18:06,780
States contributed significantly to a growing movement in literature toward realism

191
00:18:06,780 --> 00:18:13,500
and naturalism. After the 1870s, a number of important authors began to reject the

192
00:18:13,500 --> 00:18:21,040
romanticism that had prevailed immediately following the Civil War of 1861 to 1865,

193
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:23,760
and turned instead to realism.

194
00:18:24,460 --> 00:18:29,520
Determined to portray life as it was, with loyalty to real life and accurate

195
00:18:29,520 --> 00:18:36,160
representation without any idealization, they studied local dialects, wrote stories

196
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:41,740
which focused on life in specific regions of the country, and emphasized the true

197
00:18:41,740 --> 00:18:43,820
relationships between people.

198
00:18:44,280 --> 00:18:49,300
In doing so, they reflected broader trends in the society, such as

199
00:18:49,300 --> 00:18:53,940
industrialization, evolutionary theory, which emphasized the effect of the

200
00:18:53,940 --> 00:18:57,600
environment on humans, and the influence of science.

201
00:18:57,600 --> 00:19:04,740
Realists such as Joel Chandler Harris and Ellen Glasgow depicted life in the South,

202
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:11,080
Hamlin Garland described life on the Great Plains, and Sarah Orne Jewett wrote about

203
00:19:11,080 --> 00:19:13,180
everyday life in rural New England.

204
00:19:13,960 --> 00:19:19,540
Another realist, Brett Hart, achieved fame with stories that portrayed local life in

205
00:19:19,540 --> 00:19:21,160
the California mining camps.

206
00:19:21,940 --> 00:19:27,980
Samuel Clemens, who adopted the pen name Mark Twain, became the country's most

207
00:19:27,980 --> 00:19:33,260
outstanding realist author, observing life around him with a humorous and skeptical

208
00:19:33,260 --> 00:19:40,340
eye. In his stories and novels, Twain drew on his own experiences, and used dialect

209
00:19:40,340 --> 00:19:45,840
and common speech instead of literary language, touching off a major change in

210
00:19:45,840 --> 00:19:47,180
American prose style.

211
00:19:47,180 --> 00:19:51,280
Other writers became impatient even with realism.

212
00:19:52,040 --> 00:19:56,680
Pushing evolutionary theory to its limits, they wrote of a world in which a cruel

213
00:19:56,680 --> 00:19:59,840
and merciless environment determined human fate.

214
00:20:00,480 --> 00:20:06,260
These writers, called naturalists, often focused on economic hardship, studying

215
00:20:06,260 --> 00:20:11,700
people struggling with poverty, and other aspects of urban and industrial life.

216
00:20:11,700 --> 00:20:17,700
Naturalists brought to their writing a passion for direct and honest experience.

217
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:24,540
Theodore Dreiser, the foremost naturalist writer, in novels such as Sister Carrie,

218
00:20:25,260 --> 00:20:30,600
grimly portrayed a dark world in which human beings were tossed about by forces

219
00:20:30,600 --> 00:20:33,120
beyond their understanding or control.

220
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:38,880
Dreiser thought that writers should tell the truth about human affairs, not

221
00:20:38,880 --> 00:20:44,640
fabricate romance, and Sister Carrie, he said, was not intended as a piece of

222
00:20:44,640 --> 00:20:48,660
literary craftsmanship, but was a picture of conditions.

